Hello All3DP team,

a pitch with a five-minute test at the end of it.

Open any downloaded STL with a honeycomb pattern or a knurl in a mesh tool and
you get a few hundred separate faces. There is nothing useful to edit, because
the pattern was never stored as a structure. It is only geometry that happens
to look like one.

Solidon3D 0.5.0 recognises it as one feature, with pitch, cell width and
depth, a single entry in the object tree. One click removes it, three numbers
set it again, and the cells stay where they were. It works around a handle
too: there the pattern is measured in the unrolled surface, so the grooves are
equally deep everywhere and the circumference closes without a seam.

In case Solidon3D has not crossed your desk: it sits between the downloaded
model and the slicer. Click a hole and its dimensions appear on the model
itself, distances to the edges, centre, diameter, as fields you type into. Set
8 instead of 6 and the countersink moves with it. Pick a fit for the screw,
read the check report before you export, and send the file to the slicer you
already use.

There is no good way to do that today. In Fusion the imported STL is a lump of
triangles the CAD tools cannot grip, so you rebuild the part. The mesh tools
that could edit them are gone, Meshmixer and Microsoft's 3D Builder both
discontinued. And the parametric customisers on model platforms only help when
the author anticipated the parameter you need.

Underneath the pattern sits the real change of this release: the mesh becomes
an exact body. Holes, countersinks and threads stay real faces and edges when
you move or duplicate them, and STEP goes in and comes back out cleanly. The
options that used to switch between mesh and exact kernels are gone.

One thing your readers may care about more than any of that: clearance is not
entered as a number but as a reference to the material. You write "clearance
for PETG" instead of a value in millimetres, and switching to PLA changes the
clearance with it. Print the supplied tolerance ladder, measure it, and the
profile matches your own printer from then on.

Nothing is needed from me to try it: the demo is free and complete. Download
any model with a knurl, click the pattern in the object tree and set a
different pitch. I am happy to send print-resolution images or the models I
used.

Solidon3D runs on Windows, macOS and Linux, in six languages, with no account
and no network.

